📋 Objective
Build a multi-container application with 2 websites, a database, and a cache using Docker Compose.

📚 Textbook Chapters Studied
Chapter 4 – Docker Compose and Local Application Stacks

🎯 Theory Summary (In My Own Words)
What is Docker Compose?
Docker Compose lets you run multiple containers from a single YAML file.

Why Use Compose?
One command starts everything

Consistent across team members

Easy to share

🔧 Lab Environment
OS: Ubuntu 22.04 (WSL2)

Docker Engine: 29.1.3

Shell: Bash

📝 Files Created
Dockerfile
dockerfile
FROM nginx:alpine
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
COPY admin.html /usr/share/nginx/html/admin.html
EXPOSE 80
CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]
docker-compose.yml
yaml
version: '3.8'

services:
  main-site:
    image: multi-site:1.0
    container_name: main-site
    ports:
      - "8080:80"
    depends_on:
      - db
      - cache

  admin-site:
    image: multi-site:1.0
    container_name: admin-site
    ports:
      - "8081:80"
    depends_on:
      - db
      - cache

  db:
    image: mysql:8.0
    container_name: shared-db
    environment:
      MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD: root123
      MYSQL_DATABASE: quickshop
    ports:
      - "3306:3306"
    volumes:
      - db_data:/var/lib/mysql

  cache:
    image: redis:alpine
    container_name: shared-cache
    ports:
      - "6379:6379"

volumes:
  db_data:
🔧 Commands Used
bash
docker build -t multi-site:1.0 .
docker-compose up -d
docker-compose ps
docker-compose logs --tail 10
📊 Validation Results
Service	Port	Status
main-site	8080	✅ Running
admin-site	8081	✅ Running
shared-db	3306	✅ Running
shared-cache	6379	✅ Running
Browser Test
Main site: http://localhost:8080 ✅

Admin site: http://localhost:8081 ✅

Database Test
bash
docker exec -it shared-db mysql -u root -proot123 -e "SHOW DATABASES;"
✅ quickshop database exists

🐛 Issues Encountered
Issue: Port Already in Use
Symptom: port is already allocated
Fix: Stop the conflicting container or use a different port.

🗣️ 60-Second Interview Answer
"I used Docker Compose to run a complete application stack — two websites, a MySQL database, and a Redis cache — with one command. This is how companies run multi-service applications locally."

✅ Lessons Learned
One YAML file replaces multiple docker run commands

Services can share resources (database, cache)

Compose simplifies multi-container management

